LSF vs Slurm in one line: LSF computes priority as shares divided by weighted usage at each tree level. Slurm turns shares and usage into a normalized 0–1 factor and adds it to other weighted factors.

Grid Engine (SGE, then Univa, now Altair) is the most layered of the three. Fairshare lives inside a ticket system, and tickets are only one of three terms in the final priority.

Final job priority
prio = weight_priority × npprior
     + weight_urgency  × nurg
     + weight_ticket   × ntckts

These weights are set in qconf -msconf. Defaults: weight_priority=1.0, weight_urgency=0.1, weight_ticket=0.01.

Term	What it is
npprior	Normalized POSIX priority from qsub -p (−1023 to 1024, mapped to 0–1)
nurg	Normalized urgency
ntckts	Normalized ticket count. This is where fairshare lives.

Urgency:

urgency = rrcontr + wtcontr + dlcontr
rrcontr: resource requests × the urgency value of each complex (qconf -mc), multiplied by slots.
wtcontr: waiting_time × weight_waiting_time. This is the age factor.
dlcontr: weight_deadline / time_left_to_deadline.
Tickets: the fairshare part
tickets = share_tree_tickets + functional_tickets + override_tickets

The size of each ticket pool is set by weight_tickets_share, weight_tickets_functional, and override tickets assigned directly. policy_hierarchy (default OFS) sets the order in which the policies apply.

Share tree policy (usage-based, historical):

Long-term entitlement: each node’s share divided by its siblings’ total shares, multiplied down the tree path. It is the same idea as Slurm’s normalized shares.
Actual usage:
  usage = cpu × w_cpu + mem × w_mem + io × w_io

The weights come from usage_weight_list. The default is CPU only.

Decay: usage halves every halftime hours (default 168, i.e., 7 days). It is the counterpart of LSF’s HIST_HOURS and Slurm’s PriorityDecayHalfLife.
Short-term entitlement: the long-term entitlement adjusted up or down depending on how far usage is below or above target. Underusers get more tickets and overusers get fewer.
compensation_factor: caps how far that adjustment can go. With a value of 5, a node that has been idle can’t get more than 5× its long-term share. This stops one returning user from flooding the cluster.

Functional policy (current, no history):

Splits weight_tickets_functional by functional shares across user, project, department, and job.
The split is controlled by weight_user, weight_project, weight_department, and weight_job.
It has no memory of past usage. It only reflects what is running and pending now.

Override: tickets given manually to a user, project, or job, for a temporary boost.

One way to frame it: share tree is “fair over the past week,” functional is “fair right now,” and override is “admin says so.”

“Delay is creeping”: what to tweak

1. Some users’ or projects’ jobs keep waiting longer (priority problem)

Lower halftime. Past usage is forgiven faster.
Lower compensation_factor. Use this if one returning heavy user swings priority too hard.
Shift ticket pools. Increase weight_tickets_functional relative to weight_tickets_share for less history-driven behavior.
Raise weight_waiting_time or weight_urgency so aging jobs eventually win.
Watch the ticket weight. weight_ticket is tiny by default (0.01). If you’ve raised it, tickets may now drown out everything else.
Adjust node shares in the share tree with qconf -mstree.
Diagnose:
qstat -pri shows how each job’s priority breaks down.
qstat -urg shows the urgency components.
qstat -ext shows the tickets.
sge_share_mon shows share tree usage versus entitlement live.

2. qmaster or scheduler is slow (latency problem)

report_pjob_tickets=false. Computing and reporting tickets for every pending job is expensive with large queues. This is a classic fix.
max_functional_jobs_to_schedule: limits how many pending jobs get functional tickets each pass.
max_pending_tasks_per_job: large array jobs otherwise blow up scheduling work.
reprioritize_interval=0: disables dynamic renicing of running jobs, which cuts execd/qmaster chatter.
Scheduling cadence: schedule_interval, flush_submit_sec, and flush_finish_sec. Setting the flush values too low makes the scheduler run on every submit, which hurts high submit rates.
max_reservation: resource reservation (-R y) is expensive. Keep it small.
params PROFILE=1 (and MONITOR=1) in the scheduler config logs time per scheduling phase. This shows whether ticket calculation is actually the bottleneck.

qmaster side:

Thread count: set worker, listener, and (in Univa/Altair) reader threads. Reader threads keep qstat floods off the main path.
Spooling: classic spooling, or BDB on slow or NFS storage, is a common hidden cause of submission latency.
Health check: qping -info <qmaster> <port> qmaster 1 shows thread status and message backlog.

A good one-line interview answer: “Grid Engine priority is POSIX priority plus urgency plus tickets, and fairshare is the share tree portion of the tickets. For starvation, I’d use qstat -pri to see which term dominates, then tune halftime, compensation_factor, and the share/functional ticket balance. For latency, the big wins are report_pjob_tickets=false, limiting functional ticket calculation, and fixing flush intervals and spooling.”

